ions between shape spread and size.\n3. Use log-transformations to reduce skewness in features with wide ranges, improving model robustness and interpretability.\n\nBy incorporating these engineered features, the downstream model may better distinguish between similar vehicle types while capturing meaningful patterns in silhouette geometry.", "python\n# COMPACTNESS_DISTANCE_RATIO\n# Feature description: Ratio of COMPACTNESS to DISTANCE_CIRCULARITY.\n# Usefulness: This feature captures the relationship between compactness and how the shape's area is distributed in relation to its border. Different vehicle types may exhibit unique ratios based on their structural geometry.\ndf['COMPACTNESS_DISTANCE_RATIO'] = df['COMPACTNESS'] / (df['DISTANCE_CIRCULARITY'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\npython\n# NORMALIZED_RADIUS_RATIO\n# Feature description: RADIUS_RATIO normalized by dividing it by PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO.\n# Usefulness: By combining RADIUS_RATIO with PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO, this feature adds insight into how the variation in radius compares to the aspect ratio of the vehicle’s primary axes. This can help differentiate vehicles with disproportionate geometries, like buses or vans, from compact cars.\ndf['NORMALIZED_RADIUS_RATIO'] = df['RADIUS_RATIO'] / (df['PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\npython\n# LOG_SCATTER_RATIO\n# Feature description: Log transformation of SCATTER_RATIO.\n# Usefulness: The log transformation reduces skewness in SCATTER_RATIO, making the feature better-scaled and more robust to outliers. This can help highlight subtle differences in the ratio of inertia about minor and major axes across vehicle types.\ndf['LOG_SCATTER_RATIO'] = np.log(df['SCATTER_RATIO'] + 1)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column SCATTER_RATIO is dropped\n# SCATTER_RATIO is directly transformed into LOG_SCATTER_RATIO, which provides a better-scaled version of this feature that can still capture the same relationships in the data. Keeping both may introduce redundancy.\ndf.drop(columns