ess and amplifies fine differences.\ndf['LOG_SCALED_VARIANCE_RATIO'] = np.log((df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS'] + 1) / (df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS'] + 1e-5))\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Columns (if necessary):\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column ELONGATEDNESS is dropped\n# 'ELONGATEDNESS' may provide overlapping information with other features like 'PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO' or 'SCATTER_RATIO'. Since the relationships are captured in more explicitly meaningful ways using engineered features, this column may add noise or redundancy.\ndf.drop(columns=['ELONGATEDNESS'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Explanation of Steps:\n1. Feature 1 creates a new ratio between COMPACTNESS and DISTANCE_CIRCULARITY to capture proportional geometric traits of the vehicle shapes.\n2. Feature 2 normalizes SCALED_RADIUS_OF_GYRATION using another major axis-related feature to incorporate the relative distribution of mass/spread.\n3. Feature 3 logs the ratio of variances to magnify shape asymmetries in a way that might align better with downstream classification tasks.\n4. Dropping ELONGATEDNESS is justified because it overlaps conceptually with existing features and may not offer additional predictive power after engineered features are added.\n\nThese new features can help the model differentiate between similar vehicle types, such as OPEL and SAAB, or larger vehicles like BUS and VAN, leading to better predictive performance.", "python\n# SKEWNESS_MINOR_MAJOR_RATIO\n# Feature description: Ratio of SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS to SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MAJOR_AXIS.\n# Usefulness: This feature captures the relative asymmetry between the minor and major axes. Vehicles like OPEL or SAAB may have more balanced skewness, while BUS or VAN might display a stronger asymmetry, aiding in classification.\ndf['SKEWNESS_MINOR_MAJOR_RATIO'] = df['SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'] / (df['SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MAJOR_AXIS'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\npython\n# NORMALIZED_HOLLOWS_RATIO\n# Feature description: Normalize HOLLOWS_RATIO by dividing it by COMPACTNESS.\n# Usefulness: This feature combines information about hollows and compactness to reveal structural details, which may differentiate larger, hollow-like vehicles (like VAN or BUS) from compact ones (like OPEL or SAAB).\ndf['NORMALIZED_HOLLOWS_RATIO'] = df['HOLLOWS_RATIO'] / (df['COMPACTNESS'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\npython\n# LOG_SCATTER_ELONGATEDNESS\n# Feature description: Log transformation of SCATTER_ELONGATEDNESS_RATIO.\n# Usefulness: The log transformation reduces the impact of larger variations in the ratio of SCATTER_RATIO to ELONGATEDNESS. This can better emphasize subtle differences in the shape characteristics of different vehicle types.\ndf['LOG_SCATTER_ELONGATEDNESS'] = np.log(df['SCATTER_ELONGATEDNESS_RATIO'] + 1)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column SCATTER_RATIO is dropped\n# SCATTER_RATIO might overlap in utility with LOG_SCATTER_ELONGATEDNESS, which refines its information by emphasizing the relationship with ELONGATEDNESS. Keeping both may introduce redundancy.\ndf.drop(columns=['SCATTER_RATIO'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS is directly used in the derived feature SKEWNESS_MINOR_MAJOR_RATIO, which provides more meaningful context by calculating a relative ratio. Therefore, the original column becomes less impactful on its own.\ndf.drop(columns=['SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column HOLLOWS_RATIO is dropped\n# HOLLOWS_RATIO is now normalized into NORMALIZED_HOLLOWS_RATIO, which provides a more informative feature by combining it with COMPACTNESS for added context. The original column becomes redundant.\ndf.drop(columns=['HOLLOWS_RATIO'], inplace=True)\nend", "Here are three simple, intuitive feature engineering steps that could improve the downstream classification model's performance. These steps are based on leveraging interactions between existing features and mitigating skewness in the data.\n\n---\n\npython\n# CIRCULARITY_DISTANCE_RATIO\n# Feature description: Ratio of CIRCULARITY to DISTANCE_CIRCULARITY.\n# Usefulness: This captures the relationship between how circular an object is and its border characteristics. The ratio could emphasize subtle geometric differences between vehicle types, like compactness versus border spread.\ndf['CIRCULARITY_DISTANCE_RATIO'] = df['CIRCULARITY'] / (df['DISTANCE_CIRCULARITY'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\npython\n# NORMALIZED_SCATTER_RATIO\n# Feature description: SCATTER_RATIO normalized by ELONGATEDNESS.\n# Usefulness: This feature combines elongation and scatter characteristics to understand spread versus linear shape. Larger vehicles like BUS and VAN might have distinct scatter-to-elongation relationships compared to compact shapes like OPEL or SAAB.\ndf['NORMALIZED_SCATTER_RATIO'] = df['SCATTER_RATIO'] / (df['ELONGATEDNESS'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\npython\n# CIRCULARITY_LOG\n# Feature description: Natural log transformation of CIRCULARITY.\n# Usefulness: Taking the log reduces skewness in the CIRCULARITY distribution, emphasizing differences in smaller values while maintaining sensitivity to larger values. This transformation can enhance the feature's interpretability for downstream classification.\ndf['CIRCULARITY_LOG'] = np.log(df['CIRCULARITY'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Redundant Columns (if applicable):\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column COMPACTNESS_SQRT is dropped\n# 'COMPACTNESS_SQRT' may provide overlapping information with the original COMPACTNESS column and does not add significant new value after introducing other transformed features like CIRCULARITY_LOG.\ndf.drop(columns=['COMPACTNESS_SQRT'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Final Notes:\nThese new features aim to:\n1. Highlight proportional relationships between geometric features, such as CIRCULARITY and DISTANCE_CIRCULARITY.\n2. Normalize scatter-related features to account for the impact of elongation, capturing interactions between shape spread and size.\n3. Use log-transformations to reduce skewness in features with wide ranges, improving model robustness and interpretability.\n\nBy incorporating these engineered features, the downstream model may better distinguish between similar vehicle types while capturing meaningful patterns in silhouette geometry.", "python\n# COMPACTNESS_DISTANCE_RATIO\n# Feature description: Ratio of COMPACTNESS to DISTANCE_CIRCULARITY.\n# Usefulness: This feature captures the relationship between compactness and how the shape's area is distributed in relation to its border. Different vehicle types may exhibit unique ratios based on their structural geometry.\ndf['COMPACTNESS_DISTANCE_RATIO'] = df['COMPACTNESS'] / (df['DISTANCE_CIRCULARITY'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\npython\n# NORMALIZED_RADIUS_RATIO\n# Feature description: RADIUS_RATIO normalized by dividing it by PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO.\n# Usefulness: By combining RADIUS_RATIO with PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO, this feature adds insight into how the variation in radius compares to the aspect ratio of the vehicle’s primary axes. This can help differentiate vehicles with disproportionate geometries, like buses or vans, from compact cars.\ndf['NORMALIZED_RADIUS_RATIO'] = df['RADIUS_RATIO'] / (df['PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\npython\n# LOG_SCATTER_RATIO\n# Feature description: Log transformation of SCATTER_RATIO.\n# Usefulness: The log transformation reduces skewness in SCATTER_RATIO, making the feature better-scaled and more robust to outliers. This can help highlight subtle differences in the ratio of inertia about minor and major axes across vehicle types.\ndf['LOG_SCATTER_RATIO'] = np.log(df['SCATTER_RATIO'] + 1)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column SCATTER_RATIO is dropped\n# SCATTER_RATIO is directly transformed into LOG_SCATTER_RATIO, which provides a better-scaled version of this feature that can still capture the same relationships in the data. Keeping both may introduce redundancy.\ndf.drop(columns=['SCATTER_RATIO'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column RADIUS_RATIO is dropped\n# RADIUS_RATIO is encapsulated in NORMALIZED_RADIUS_RATIO, which provides more meaningful insight by considering its relationship with PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO. The original RADIUS_RATIO column becomes less informative on its own.\ndf.drop(columns=['RADIUS_RATIO'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column DISTANCE_CIRCULARITY is dropped\n# DISTANCE_CIRCULARITY is represented in COMPACTNESS_DISTANCE_RATIO, which adds a proportional comparison with COMPACTNESS for more detailed geometric insights. The standalone column is likely redundant.\ndf.drop(columns=['DISTANCE_CIRCULARITY'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n### Final Notes:\n- These engineered features are simple and intuitive, leveraging proportional relationships and transformations to better capture geometric variations.\n- Dropping redundant columns ensures the dataset remains clean and avoids multicollinearity, while preserving the useful information in transformed features.", "### Possible Next Three Steps for Feature Engineering ###\nThe goal is to improve the downstream classification performance by generating simple, intuitive, and meaningful features based on the dataset's characteristics.\n\n---\n\npython\n# VARIANCE_RATIO\n# Feature description: Ratio of SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS to SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS.\n# Usefulness: This feature captures how the variance along the minor axis compares to the variance along the major axis, which can help distinguish between vehicles with different proportions (e.g., elongated vehicles like buses vs. compact vehicles like OPEL or SAAB).\ndf['VARIANCE_RATIO'] = df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS'] / (df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\npython\n# NORMALIZED_COMPACTNESS\n# Feature description: COMPACTNESS normalized by dividing it by PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO.\n# Usefulness: This feature adds insight into how compactness relates to the aspect ratio of the principal axes, providing an adjusted view of shape proportions that may differ across vehicle types.\ndf['NORMALIZED_COMPACTNESS'] = df['COMPACTNESS'] / (df['PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\npython\n# LOG_HOLLOWS_RATIO\n# Feature description: Log transformation of HOLLOWS_RATIO.\n# Usefulness: The log transformation reduces the impact of extreme values in HOLLOWS_RATIO, highlighting smaller variations important for distinguishing compact vehicles (e.g., OPEL, SAAB) from larger or hollow-shaped ones (e.g., VAN, BUS).\ndf['LOG_HOLLOWS_RATIO'] = np.log(df['HOLLOWS_RATIO'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Redundant Columns ###\nOnce the new features are added, old features that are encapsulated or redundant can be removed to simplify the dataset and avoid multicollinearity.\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column HOLLOWS_RATIO is dropped\n# HOLLOWS_RATIO is transformed into LOG_HOLLOWS_RATIO, which better captures variations in the data and handles extreme values. The original feature is no longer needed.\ndf.drop(columns=['HOLLOWS_RATIO'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column COMPACTNESS is dropped\n# COMPACTNESS is represented in NORMALIZED_COMPACTNESS, which provides a more meaningful relationship by adjusting for PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO. The original feature becomes redundant.\ndf.drop(columns=['COMPACTNESS'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS is used in VARIANCE_RATIO, which provides relational insight by comparing it to SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS. The original column is no longer needed.\ndf.drop(columns=['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Final Notes ###\n- The engineered features focus on proportional relationships and mitigate skewness while incorporating geometric insights that align with the dataset's objective.\n- Redundant columns are dropped in favor of transformed features that preserve or enhance their original information.\n- These steps aim to improve class separability for vehicle types, enabling better classification model performance.", "### Possible Next Three Steps for Feature Engineering\n\nBelow are three simple, direct, and intuitive feature engineering steps aimed at improving the downstream classification model's performance. These features leverage proportional relationships or transformations to capture meaningful patterns in silhouette geometry.\n\n---\n\npython\n# FEATURE 1: COMPACTNESS_RATIO\n# Feature description: Ratio of COMPACTNESS to PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO.\n# Usefulness: This feature measures the relationship between compactness and the aspect ratio of the vehicle's primary axes. Since compact cars (e.g., OPEL, SAAB) may exhibit higher compactness relative to their axis aspect ratio, while larger vehicles like VAN or BUS have lower values, this ratio provides additional context for classification.\ndf['COMPACTNESS_RATIO'] = df['COMPACTNESS'] / (df['PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\npython\n# FEATURE 2: VARIANCE_ASYMMETRY_RATIO\n# Feature description: Ratio of SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS to SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS.\n# Usefulness: This feature captures asymmetrical variance between the major and minor axes of the silhouette. Larger vehicles like BUS or VAN might exhibit higher asymmetry compared to compact cars. This feature is useful for separating vehicles with distinct structural differences.\ndf['VARIANCE_ASYMMETRY_RATIO'] = df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS'] / (df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\npython\n# FEATURE 3: NORMALIZED_HOLLOWS_RATIO\n# Feature description: Ratio of HOLLOWS_RATIO to SCATTER_RATIO.\n# Usefulness: This feature combines information about the proportion of hollows (holes) in the shape relative to scatter. Vehicles like VAN or BUS (which might have more hollows in their silhouettes) could exhibit distinct behaviors compared to compact cars. This feature provides more nuanced insights into structural composition.\ndf['NORMALIZED_HOLLOWS_RATIO'] = df['HOLLOWS_RATIO'] / (df['SCATTER_RATIO'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Redundant Columns\n\nTo simplify the dataset and avoid multicollinearity, redundant columns that overlap with the newly created features are dropped.\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO is dropped\n# PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO is now represented in COMPACTNESS_RATIO, which combines its information with COMPACTNESS for a more meaningful feature. The original column becomes redundant.\ndf.drop(columns=['PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS is dropped\n# SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS is directly used in VARIANCE_ASYMMETRY_RATIO, which provides additional context by creating a ratio with the corresponding minor axis variance. The standalone column is no longer necessary.\ndf.drop(columns=['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column HOLLOWS_RATIO is dropped\n# HOLLOWS_RATIO is utilized in NORMALIZED_HOLLOWS_RATIO, where it is contextualized relative to SCATTER_RATIO. The new feature renders the original column less informative.\ndf.drop(columns=['HOLLOWS_RATIO'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Final Notes:\n1. These features emphasize interactions between existing geometrical attributes in the dataset to uncover patterns specific to different vehicle types.\n2. Dropping redundant columns keeps the dataset clean and avoids redundancy, ensuring better model interpretability.\n3. These additions and deletions are intuitive and directly linked to the physical characteristics of vehicle silhouettes, which aligns with the dataset's task of vehicle classification."]###,
